1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,822
Aarav's meter reads seven
volts from a small adapter.

2
00:00:03,822 --> 00:00:07,354
But is that voltage steady,
or wobbling up and down?

3
00:00:07,704 --> 00:00:09,591
A meter gives just one number.

4
00:00:09,591 --> 00:00:12,256
To see the shape, he
needs an oscilloscope.

5
00:00:12,756 --> 00:00:15,775
An oscilloscope draws
a voltage as a picture.

6
00:00:15,775 --> 00:00:18,278
Voltage goes up and down the screen.

7
00:00:18,278 --> 00:00:19,787
Time runs across it.

8
00:00:20,437 --> 00:00:23,667
The classic one is the
cathode ray oscilloscope.

9
00:00:23,667 --> 00:00:26,113
Its heart is the cathode ray tube.

10
00:00:26,463 --> 00:00:28,817
At the back sits the electron gun.

11
00:00:28,817 --> 00:00:31,261
A heated cathode gives off electrons.

12
00:00:31,611 --> 00:00:34,086
The control grid sets
how many get through.

13
00:00:34,086 --> 00:00:35,581
That sets the brightness.

14
00:00:35,931 --> 00:00:39,798
Anodes speed the electrons up
and focus them into a thin beam.

15
00:00:40,148 --> 00:00:44,599
The beam hits a screen coated with
phosphor, which glows as a bright spot.

16
00:00:45,249 --> 00:00:47,985
Two pairs of deflection
plates steer the beam.

17
00:00:47,985 --> 00:00:51,794
The vertical plates move it up
and down, with the input signal.

18
00:00:52,144 --> 00:00:56,301
The horizontal plates sweep it from
left to right at a steady speed, then

19
00:00:56,301 --> 00:00:57,269
snap it back.

20
00:00:57,269 --> 00:00:59,617
That sawtooth comes from the time base.

21
00:00:59,967 --> 00:01:03,092
Put the two together, and
the spot draws the waveform.

22
00:01:03,442 --> 00:01:07,329
A trigger circuit starts every sweep
at the same point on the wave, so

23
00:01:07,329 --> 00:01:08,835
the picture stands still.

24
00:01:09,486 --> 00:01:14,084
A dual channel oscilloscope shows two
signals at once, on channel one and

25
00:01:14,084 --> 00:01:15,058
channel two.

26
00:01:15,408 --> 00:01:18,388
Most use one beam and
an electronic switch.

27
00:01:18,738 --> 00:01:23,137
In alternate mode, it draws channel
one on one sweep, and channel two on

28
00:01:23,137 --> 00:01:24,037
the next.

29
00:01:23,906 --> 00:01:25,490
This suits fast signals.

30
00:01:25,840 --> 00:01:30,228
In chop mode, it hops between the
two many times during every sweep.

31
00:01:30,228 --> 00:01:31,865
This suits slow signals.

32
00:01:32,515 --> 00:01:34,407
Let's use it on the breadboard.

33
00:01:34,757 --> 00:01:37,964
An AC signal feeds a
resistor and a capacitor.

34
00:01:37,964 --> 00:01:40,040
Channel one probes the input.

35
00:01:40,040 --> 00:01:42,323
Channel two probes the capacitor.

36
00:01:42,673 --> 00:01:43,978
Watch both traces.

37
00:01:43,978 --> 00:01:47,463
The output is smaller, and
it lags behind the input.

38
00:01:48,113 --> 00:01:49,632
Turn up the frequency.

39
00:01:49,632 --> 00:01:52,968
The output shrinks even
more, and lags even further.

40
00:01:53,618 --> 00:01:55,619
Now, how do we read the screen?

41
00:01:55,619 --> 00:01:56,990
Two knobs matter most.

42
00:01:57,340 --> 00:02:00,235
Volts per division sets
the vertical scale.

43
00:02:00,235 --> 00:02:03,182
Time per division sets
the horizontal scale.

44
00:02:03,832 --> 00:02:08,445
Peak to peak voltage equals the number
of vertical divisions, times volts

45
00:02:08,445 --> 00:02:09,486
per division.

46
00:02:10,186 --> 00:02:15,144
The time period equals the divisions
in one cycle, times time per division.

47
00:02:15,144 --> 00:02:17,704
And frequency is one over the period.

48
00:02:18,404 --> 00:02:21,972
With two channels, you can
read the phase difference too.

49
00:02:21,972 --> 00:02:26,469
It is the shift in divisions, over the
divisions in one cycle, times three

50
00:02:26,469 --> 00:02:28,149
hundred and sixty degrees.

51
00:02:28,849 --> 00:02:29,986
Worked example.

52
00:02:29,986 --> 00:02:34,898
A wave is four divisions tall, peak
to peak, at two volts per division.

53
00:02:35,248 --> 00:02:39,599
So the peak to peak voltage is eight
volts, and the peak is four volts.

54
00:02:40,199 --> 00:02:45,545
One cycle spans five divisions, at zero
point two milliseconds per division.

55
00:02:45,545 --> 00:02:47,466
So T is one millisecond.

56
00:02:48,066 --> 00:02:50,826
The frequency is one over one millisecond.

57
00:02:50,826 --> 00:02:52,720
That is one thousand hertz.

58
00:02:53,370 --> 00:02:55,094
A meter gives you a number.

59
00:02:55,094 --> 00:02:57,620
An oscilloscope gives
you the whole picture.

60
00:02:58,420 --> 00:02:59,885
Now, the exam trap.

61
00:03:00,235 --> 00:03:04,382
Many students read the full height
of the wave as the peak voltage.

62
00:03:04,732 --> 00:03:05,661
It is not.

63
00:03:05,661 --> 00:03:08,449
Top to bottom is the peak to peak value.

64
00:03:08,449 --> 00:03:10,207
The peak is half of it.

65
00:03:10,857 --> 00:03:12,364
Where is this asked?

66
00:03:12,364 --> 00:03:13,871
In PUC board papers.

67
00:03:13,871 --> 00:03:18,595
The block diagram, what each control
does, chop and alternate modes, and

68
00:03:18,595 --> 00:03:21,599
reading voltage and
frequency from the screen.

69
00:03:22,249 --> 00:03:23,149
Try this.

70
00:03:23,085 --> 00:03:27,072
A scope is set to zero point
five milliseconds per division.

71
00:03:27,072 --> 00:03:31,742
One cycle of each wave covers eight
divisions, and channel two is shifted

72
00:03:31,742 --> 00:03:33,934
one division behind channel one.

73
00:03:33,934 --> 00:03:36,861
Find the frequency and
the phase difference.

74
00:03:42,261 --> 00:03:43,161
A.

75
00:03:42,677 --> 00:03:46,215
Two hundred and fifty hertz,
and forty five degrees.

76
00:03:46,565 --> 00:03:50,932
The period is eight divisions
times zero point five milliseconds.

77
00:03:50,932 --> 00:03:52,839
That is four milliseconds.

78
00:03:53,189 --> 00:03:57,968
So the frequency is one over four
milliseconds, two hundred and fifty hertz.

79
00:03:58,318 --> 00:04:00,878
The shift is one division out of eight.

80
00:04:00,878 --> 00:04:05,417
So the phase is one eighth of three
hundred and sixty, which is forty five

81
00:04:05,417 --> 00:04:06,317
degrees.

82
00:04:06,745 --> 00:04:08,269
Screenshot this card.

83
00:04:11,469 --> 00:04:15,132
Next, measuring voltage, current,
resistance and waveforms.

84
00:04:15,132 --> 00:04:17,141
Every instrument, put to work.
